<UserControl x:Class="SsmsDataAnalyzer.Vsix.ResultsGrid.GridFindView"
             x:ClassModifier="internal"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:vsp="clr-namespace:Microsoft.VisualStudio.PlatformUI;assembly=Microsoft.VisualStudio.Shell.15.0"
             HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Top"
             Background="{DynamicResource {x:Static vsp:EnvironmentColors.ToolWindowBackgroundBrushKey}}"
             FocusManager.FocusedElement="{Binding ElementName=SearchTextBox}">
    <!--
        v0.7.2: results-grid Find moved from a floating WPF Window to a real VS
        ToolWindowPane (see GridFindToolWindow) after the floating window proved unable to
        reliably own keyboard focus/routing inside the SSMS host — typing, Backspace and
        Enter were reaching the SQL editor underneath instead of this control, and Ctrl+F
        turned out to be intercepted by VS's own Find and Replace before it ever reached the
        results grid. A real ToolWindowPane sidesteps both: VS owns focus and keyboard
        routing for its own tool windows, so this control just needs ordinary WPF event
        handlers, no WindowInteropHelper/Activate/Keyboard.Focus workarounds at all.

        Same lean widget shape as before: text box, explicit Find button (never
        search-as-you-type — a keystroke must not launch a full chunked scan), N-of-M
        counter, prev/next. No Close button — a VS tool window closes via its own tab, not
        ours; no options panel, no regex (lead's "keep it lean" still applies).

        v0.7.3 field report: "look of search... wierd" — a screenshot showed the row
        vertically centred in a mostly-empty pane with two oversized grey rectangles where
        Previous/Next should be. Root cause, confirmed by inspection rather than guessed: the
        old StackPanel (root) had no explicit VerticalAlignment, so it defaulted to Stretch
        and filled the pane's full height; FindButton/PreviousButton/NextButton/CancelButton
        likewise had no VerticalAlignment (only SearchTextBox/CounterText did), so THEY
        defaulted to Stretch too and rendered as full-height blocks rather than normal
        buttons. Fixed here by anchoring both this control and its single content row to the
        TOP (HorizontalAlignment/VerticalAlignment on the UserControl itself, plus the same
        on the Grid below) and giving every single element in the row an explicit
        VerticalAlignment="Center" — nothing is left to default to Stretch this time.

        Layout is a Grid, not a StackPanel, so the search box can be given a star-sized
        column that actually fills available width when docked wide, while every other
        column stays Auto (sized to its own content) — this is what makes the row behave
        correctly docked narrow, docked wide, AND floated, per the lead's explicit ask to
        test resize rather than assume a StackPanel (which has no concept of "fill remaining
        width") would do the same. Background uses EnvironmentColors.ToolWindowBackgroundBrushKey
        (VS's actual tool-window chrome colour, confirmed to exist by decompiling
        Microsoft.VisualStudio.Shell.15.0.dll's EnvironmentColors type) rather than
        SystemColors.WindowBrushKey (the plain white "document surface" colour the old XAML
        used, and exactly what the user's screenshot showed looking wrong against SSMS's own
        chrome) — same vsp: alias/assembly ProfileView.xaml already uses for its own
        VS-theme-aware brushes, so this follows that file's convention rather than inventing
        a new one.

        Headless-verified (RenderHarness gridfind-layout mode, loading this markup directly
        via XamlReader with x:Class/event attributes stripped, since GridFindView.xaml.cs's
        constructor needs a live VS host for its theme-colour lookups): the row stays a short
        (~22px), top-anchored strip with normal-sized buttons at 900px, 340px and 260px wide,
        and the search box actually grows/shrinks with the available width instead of staying
        a fixed 180px.

        A ScrollViewer wrap was tried first for the narrowest-dock case (so an overflowing
        button could be scrolled to instead of clipped) and REJECTED after the same harness
        caught it regressing the "fills available width" requirement: a ScrollViewer offers
        its content effectively infinite width during measure, which collapses a Grid's star
        column to its MinWidth at every window size, not just the narrow one (confirmed: the
        search box stayed pinned to 80px wide even at 900px window width). Plain Grid clipping
        at the narrowest docked widths is the accepted trade-off instead — the same behaviour
        VS's own toolbars/toolwindow chrome show when a pane is docked narrower than its
        content, rather than losing "fills available width" at every size to fix a
        narrow-only edge case.
    -->
    <Grid HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="6" KeyDown="Root_KeyDown">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" MinWidth="60" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <TextBox x:Name="SearchTextBox" Grid.Column="0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 Margin="0,0,6,0" TextChanged="SearchTextBox_TextChanged" />
        <Button x:Name="FindButton" Grid.Column="1" Content="Find" Click="FindButton_Click"
                ToolTip="Search (Enter)" Padding="8,2" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,8,0" />
        <TextBlock x:Name="CounterText" Grid.Column="2" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   MinWidth="90" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.GrayTextBrushKey}}" />
        <Button x:Name="PreviousButton" Grid.Column="3" Content="Previous" Click="PreviousButton_Click"
                ToolTip="Previous match (Shift+F3 / Shift+Enter)" Padding="8,2" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0" />
        <Button x:Name="NextButton" Grid.Column="4" Content="Next" Click="NextButton_Click"
                ToolTip="Next match (F3 / Enter)" Padding="8,2" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,4,0" />
        <Button x:Name="CancelButton" Grid.Column="5" Content="Cancel" Click="CancelButton_Click"
                ToolTip="Cancel the current search" Padding="8,2" VerticalAlignment="Center" Visibility="Collapsed" />
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
